::Q1::What does OLTP stand for? 
{
~Operational Logic and Table Processing
~Organized Long-Term Processing
=Online Transaction Processing
~Online Table Processing
}

::Q2::What workload is OLAP optimized for? 
{
=Large-scale queries that aggregate and analyze many records
~Small real-time updates to individual records
~Single-row transaction processing
~Password entry by application users
}

::Q3::Why do OLAP systems favor read-heavy access patterns? 
{
=They are queried frequently for analysis and reporting and are updated less often
~They are designed primarily for changing one row at a time
~They exist only to validate passwords
~They cannot store historical information
}

::Q4::Why are OLTP databases commonly structured to reduce duplicate data? 
{
~OLTP systems are designed to maximize duplicated records
~Normalization is used only for analytical dashboards
=Normalization supports accurate, precise updates and reduces unnecessary duplication
~Duplicate data is required for every transaction
}

::Q5::Why is a data warehouse considered an OLAP system? 
{
=It is designed to support analytical queries over large amounts of data
~It stores only temporary application sessions
~It is designed primarily to update one transaction at a time
~It cannot perform aggregations
}